          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Nikki directly avows a present loving emotional state. The narration explicitly characterizes her words as an honest admission, making truth-state presentation the expression’s clear dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"She turned to him, her heart full. “I love you,” she said suddenly, the words spilling out before she could stop them.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"The admission felt like a release, a moment of pure honesty\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The expression contains no undertaking by Nikki to perform an action, maintain the relationship, or assume a defined obligation. Keith reciprocates the avowal, but neither utterance establishes explicit commitment terms; later discussion of avoiding a final goodbye is separate.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"“I love you too,” he said, his voice steady and sincere.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Alongside its avowal force, the expression is represented as a spontaneous, affect-driven discharge: it comes suddenly and spills out before Nikki can inhibit it. This provides substantial evidence of reflex-like expressive force rather than mere emotional delivery.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"“I love you,” she said suddenly, the words spilling out before she could stop them.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"The admission felt like a release\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"No core force beyond avowal and spontaneous expressive release is supported. In particular, the expression is not framed as a question, request, command, greeting, or other distinct act.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Nikki and Keith have spent an intimate night together, they are lying in bed. With Keith tracing circles on her arm, Nikki turns toward him and spontaneously says the target. Keith pauses, smiles, and reciprocates with “I love you too.”\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The target itself undertakes no specified action or relational obligation. Keith immediately recognizes and reciprocates the loving avowal, so there is clear interpersonal uptake of the admission, but that reciprocal response should not be treated as acceptance of a separate promise.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not recognize the supplied passage as a particular known work from the provided text.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied evidence strongly supports Nikki’s represented affection and the immediate reciprocal response. It does not settle any formal relationship status, long-term commitment terms, or eventual durability of the relationship.\"}"
